    "source_text": "Worked example (small and large errors). Residuals that are $\\mathcal N(0,0.5^2)$ with probability $0.8$ and $\\mathcal N(0,3^2)$ with probability $0.2$ (the \"80% small, 20% large\" option of the Module 15 explorer) have variance $0.8\\cdot0.25+0.2\\cdot9=2$. Their tail is nothing like that of $\\mathcal N(0,2)$: $\\mathbb P(|E|\\gt5)=0.8\\cdot\\mathbb P(|Z|\\gt10)+0.2\\cdot\\mathbb P(|Z|\\gt\\tfrac53)\\approx0.019$, against $0.0004$ for the Gaussian with the same variance, while near zero the mixture is more concentrated ($\\mathbb P(|E|\\gt1)\\approx0.18$ against $0.48$). An ensemble of $E$ Gaussian predictions is an equal-weight mixture: its covariance is the average member covariance (aleatoric part) plus the covariance of the member means (epistemic part, the $\\hat\\Sigma$ of Section 1).",
    "text_sha256": "3e11eafef313d223dc6d2f9114c2c04c2a8cd1ddb18219b93181860f3c899a0b",
